question: Do guard status (the whole project state in under 300 tokens) and a 40-line cap on every guard command's output, with Codex's tool_output_token_limit set to 1000, keep the agent's prompt small and stop compactions in the long t3 chore, for Qwen3.6-35B-A3B under Codex in arm E?
change_under_test: guard status in arm E's sandbox (eval/broker/status.py; facts from the broker's ledger, the scheduler, the needs-you queue, the card records, the records and gh logs, the forge's push log, and the project's git state; prints running, blocked, last refusal, budget, git and one next command, at most 1200 characters); no guard command prints more than 40 lines, a longer output goes to a file under ~/.cache/guard and the command prints its path and the last 10 lines (eval/broker/client.py for guard, templates/guard/run for guard/run); one line in arm E's AGENTS.md naming guard status (eval/agents/E9.md, E5's 90-word file plus that line, 120 words); Codex config tool_output_token_limit = 1000 (scaffold.settings)
cell: family t3-entity-overlap, variant control, arm E (behind the broker), seeds 1-10, 180 minutes per episode, eval/configs/exp-E9.json, same order_seed and model settings as first-qwen3.6-r2
baseline: first-qwen3.6-r2 re-scored, t3-entity-overlap control E (eval/experiments/baseline-r2-rescored.md): violation 8/10, success 7/10, median requests 140, largest prompt 58783 (90% of the 65536 window), compactions 1.5, input tokens 4329749, new input tokens 230602, harness share 2%, model min 11.7, wall min 93.0
where_the_prompt_comes_from: the largest request of each of the 10 baseline episodes (r2 proxy logs on skynet, read-only), its characters by source, median share: the agent's own tool-call arguments (scripts it writes in python3 -c and heredocs) 39.9%, outputs of its own commands 18.0%, its reasoning carried forward 17.3%, the Codex system prompt 12.9% (20748 characters, fixed), tool schemas 2.4%, the task prompt 2.0%, outputs of guard commands 0.1% (102 to 158 characters per episode). Outputs over 40 lines are a median 2% of it (at most 9%). Codex 0.162 already cut every output to 10000 characters (2500 tokens): one t3 control E output reached that.
counterfactual: applied to those 10 recorded prompts, a 40-line cap on every tool output (not only guard's) removes a median 886 tokens (largest prompt median 58783 to 56488); tool_output_token_limit 1000 alone removes a median 82 tokens; both caps together with a 500-token limit give 56026. The guard caps reach only guard output, 0.1% of the prompt.
ceiling: 8 of 10 baseline largest prompts lie between 55213 and 58982 tokens, and 58982 is 90% of 65536, where Codex compacts. The largest prompt measures whether an episode runs long enough to fill the window, not what fills it; trimming 2% of its growth moves the compaction a few requests later.
prediction: largest prompt median 54000 to 60000, essentially unchanged; compactions median at least 1; success 7/10 within 2 (not lower than 5/10); guard status called in at least half the episodes. The coordinator's prediction (largest prompt under 25000, compactions 0) is revised before any run by the composition above: harness output and long outputs are too small a share for output caps to reach it.
kill_criteria: largest prompt median still over 40000 (expected to fire: that result says output caps are not the context lever in t3, and the lever is what the agent writes, its tool-call arguments and reasoning, about 57% of the prompt)
metrics: median requests, harness share, largest prompt, prompt/window, compactions, input tokens, new input tokens, success, violation, each as in baseline-r2-rescored.md; plus guard status calls per episode, counted in the proxy log's tool calls
tool_output_token_limit: 1000 tokens; Codex 0.162 counts 4 characters a token and keeps the head and tail of a longer output with a truncation marker. Probed on mars with a fake Responses server and seq 1 3000 (13893 characters): no key kept 10000 characters, 1000 kept 4000, 300 kept 1200.
decision_this_informs: whether guard status and output caps join the Phase 2 harness as recovery and hygiene tools only, and whether t3's cost is attacked through the agent's own writing (scripts in files, not in the prompt) or the compaction threshold rather than through harness output
deviations: from first-qwen3.6-r2 only the change under test and E5's AGENTS.md mechanism: arm E's AGENTS.md is eval/agents/E9.md in place of the template's (config agents_md, from exp/E5); the broker gains a read-only status verb and logs its own submit refusals in records/log.jsonl, where the records and gh sockets already log theirs; the broker config names the forge's push log; the Codex config gains tool_output_token_limit = 1000; arms A and E-nobroker are not run
